So `nameNsPush` populates `ctxt->pushTab[ctxt->nameNr]` (the metadata stack), but `namePush` (SAX1) does NOT.

Now in `xmlParseElement`, the error path:
```c
    if (CUR == 0) {
        const xmlChar *name = ctxt->nameTab[ctxt->nameNr - 1];
        int line = ctxt->pushTab[ctxt->nameNr - 1].line;  // <-- reads pushTab metadata
        ...
    }
```

This unconditionally reads `ctxt->pushTab[ctxt->nameNr - 1].line`. But in the SAX1 path, `pushTab` was never populated (it's NULL or has stale data). So `ctxt->pushTab` could be NULL, leading to a near-null read (dereferencing NULL + offset).

So the vulnerability is: SAX1 start-element path uses `namePush` which records the open name in `nameTab` but does NOT populate the parallel `pushTab` metadata stack. Then the error path in `xmlParseElement` (when CUR == 0, premature end of input) unconditionally reads `ctxt->pushTab[ctxt->nameNr - 1].line`, which dereferences a NULL (or uninitialized) `pushTab` pointer → near-null read.

So to trigger this, I need:
1. SAX1 mode (ctxt->sax2 == 0)
2. A start tag that opens an element (so nameNr > 0, name pushed via namePush)
3. Premature end of input (CUR == 0) so the error path is taken

